{"name":"Barley Field","hex":"#c7bcae","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"barley","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A cereal of the species Hordeum vulgare or its grains, often used as food or to make beer and other malted drinks.","The seed of Job's tears (Coix lacryma-jobi)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/barley"},{"term":"field","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A land area free of woodland, cities, and towns; an area of open country.","The open country near or belonging to a town or city."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To intercept or catch (a ball) and play it.","To be the team catching and throwing the ball, as opposed to hitting it."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/field"}],"entries":[{"title":"Barley","blocks":[{"text":"Barley, a member of the grass family, is a major cereal grain grown in temperate climates globally. One of the first cultivated grains, it was domesticated in the Fertile Crescent around 9000 BC, giving it nonshattering spikelets and making it much easier to harvest. Its use then spread throughout Eurasia by 2000 BC. Barley prefers relatively low temperatures and well-drained soil to grow. It is relatively tolerant of drought and soil salinity, but is less winter-hardy than wheat or rye. Wild barley may refer to H. vulgare ssp. spontaneum, but may also refer to other species in the genus Hordeum."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Barley_%28Hordeum_vulgare%29_-_United_States_National_Arboretum_-_24_May_2009.jpg/330px-Barley_%28Hordeum_vulgare%29_-_United_States_National_Arboretum_-_24_May_2009.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barley","wikidata":"Q11577"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Barley_Barn_%28cropped%29.jpg/500px-Barley_Barn_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_